
{"id":"a546efee-f38b-4a98-8640-910e58a4a165","object":"chat.completion","created":1784988132,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case['lines']\\n    trajectory = []\\n    allowed = {'thought'}\\n    error_line = None\\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        colon_idx = line.find(':')\\n        if colon_idx == -1:\\n            error_line = i + 1\\n            break\\n        prefix = line[:colon_idx].strip()\\n        content = line[colon_idx+1:].strip()\\n        if prefix == 'Thought':\\n            rec_type = 'thought'\\n        elif prefix == 'Action':\\n            rec_type = 'action'\\n        elif prefix == 'Observation':\\n            rec_type = 'observation'\\n        elif prefix == 'Final':\\n            rec_type = 'final'\\n        else:\\n            error_line = i + 1\\n            break\\n        if rec_type not in allowed:\\n            error_line = i + 1\\n            break\\n        trajectory.append({'type': rec_type, 'content': content})\\n        if rec_type == 'thought':\\n            allowed = {'action'}\\n        elif rec_type == 'action':\\n            allowed = {'observation'}\\n        elif rec_type == 'observation':\\n            allowed = {'thought', 'final'}\\n        elif rec_type == 'final':\\n            allowed = set()\\n    if error_line is not None:\\n        return {'valid': False, 'error_line': error_line, 'trajectory': trajectory}\\n    if len(lines) == 0:\\n        return {'valid': False, 'error_line': None, 'trajectory': []}\\n    if not allowed:\\n        return {'valid': True, 'error_line': None, 'trajectory': trajectory}\\n    return {'valid': False, 'error_line': len(lines), 'trajectory': trajectory}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python function `solve(case)` that implements the described ReAct parsing and validation. The case payload has a `lines` array of strings. The task is to parse interleaved Thought, Action, Observation, and Final records. The output must be a JSON object with `error_line` (null or integer), `trajectory` (array of objects with `content` and `type` strings), and `valid` (boolean). The schema requires `additionalProperties: false` at trajectory item level and top level, but we just need to return the object accordingly.\n\nThe description: \"Parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records under a registered tool schema and stop grammar.\" But there is no explicit tool schema given in the fixture. The fixture only has lines like \"Thought: inspect\", \"Action: lookup\", \"Observation: found\", \"Thought: answer\", \"Final: done\". There's no Action Input lines. The problem likely expects parsing lines that start with \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", \"Observation:\", \"Final:\". Also note \"Action Input\" is mentioned but not present. Possibly we need to treat lines that start with \"Action Input:\" as part of action? But the fixture doesn't have them.\n\nGiven the bounded objective: \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records. Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle. Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary. On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix. Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\"\n\nAlso: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: valid_turn_order, tool_schema, observation_binding, stop_semantics.\" This suggests some specific validation rules. But without a tool schema, we might assume that any action is allowed? The fixture has \"Action: lookup\", \"Thought: inspect\", \"Observation: found\". So we need to parse these lines.\n\nThe algorithm: iterate through lines. For each line, determine its type by the prefix before colon. Trim whitespace? The prefixes are likely \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", \"Observation:\", \"Final:\". But also \"Action Input:\"? But not in fixture. We'll only handle the four.\n\nWe need to enforce order: Thought -> Action -> Observation -> (repeat) or Final at end. Specifically: start with Thought. After Thought, must be Action. After Action, must be Observation. After Observation, can be either Thought (new cycle) or Final (end). Final must be only at the end and after Observation? Actually \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" Likely after an Observation or possibly after a Thought? But reasoning boundary implies after a cycle. The typical ReAct pattern: Thought, Action, Observation, then maybe more or Final. We'll assume Final can only appear when the previous step is Observation (or at start? No). So we enforce: sequence starts with Thought, then Action, then Observation (or maybe if no Observation, it's incomplete). But also \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" Means each record must have content. The line after colon is the content.\n\nWe need to return trajectory array of objects with lowercase type: \"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\". And content is the text after colon (stripped). The valid boolean is true if entire sequence is valid, else false. error_line is the 1-based line number of first violation, or null if valid. Also \"valid prefix\" meaning we return trajectory up to that point? The description: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" The trajectory should contain only records up to the violation? Or all? The output schema requires trajectory array always be present. I think it should contain the records that are valid up to the violation (including the violation? Probably not including the violating line because it's invalid). So we need to keep trajectory as we go, and on first violation, we stop and set valid false, error_line = line number (1-indexed), and trajectory is the records parsed so far (valid ones). If valid, trajectory is all records.\n\nAlso \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records\". So type is lowercase. Content is string.\n\nWe must not mutate input case. Use only standard library.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). case is a dict with key 'lines' (list of strings). Also maybe other keys like '_case_nonce' but we ignore.\n\nEdge cases: empty lines list? Should return valid false? But what is error_line? Probably first line? But there is no line. Maybe treat as violation at line 0? But 1-based line numbers. If no lines, perhaps valid false and error_line null? The specification: \"On the first violation return its one-based line\". If no lines, there is no violation? But the sequence is incomplete. I think we need to consider missing required first Thought. So if first line is not Thought, that's violation. So empty list would be violation at line 0? But line numbers are 1-based, so error_line could be null? Possibly we should treat as invalid with error_line = 1? But there is no line. Safer: if no lines, return {valid: false, error_line: null, trajectory: []}. The schema allows error_line null. However, the description says \"first violation return its one-based line\", implying there is a line. But we can handle as null.\n\nAlso lines may have extra whitespace. Parse prefix case-insensitively? The fixture has \"Thought: inspect\" with capital T. Should treat case-sensitive? Probably keep as is. We'll treat as case-sensitive matching the exact prefix + colon. However, \"Thought:\" has capital T, \"Action:\" capital A, etc. The specification says \"lowercase typed trajectory records\" meaning type strings are lowercase. But the input lines may be mixed case? The fixture uses consistent capitalization. We'll require exact match of the prefix including case. If line doesn't start with any known prefix, it's a violation.\n\nAlso note \"Action Input\" is mentioned but not in fixture. We can ignore for now. The implementation should handle only the four types.\n\nLet's design the algorithm:\n\nInitialize trajectory = []\nexpected_next = \"thought\" (first required)\nFor each line in case['lines'], 0-index i:\n  line = lines[i].strip()? The content after colon may have leading/trailing spaces. The fixture has no extra spaces. We'll not strip the whole line, but extract based on first occurrence of colon. However, lines like \"Thought: inspect\" we can split on ': ' but there may be no space. Use split(':', 1). Then type_str = left part, content = right part stripped? The content should be the text after colon, preserving internal spaces but trimming leading/trailing? The fixture has \"inspect\" no spaces. We'll strip content.\n\nNow determine record_type: lower(type_str) mapping. But type_str should be \"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\". If not, violation.\n\nCheck order: \n- If record_type == \"thought\": expected must be \"thought\" (start of cycle). After observing? Actually after observation, expected becomes \"thought\". So allow.\n- If record_type == \"action\": expected must be \"action\" (i.e., after thought).\n- If record_type == \"observation\": expected must be \"observation\" (after action).\n- If record_type == \"final\": expected must be \"final\" (allowed only after observation? Or also after thought? We'll enforce that final only allowed when expected is \"final\", which we set after observing observation? Let's define state machine:\n\nWe have states: STATE_INIT, STATE_THOUGHT, STATE_ACTION, STATE_OBSERVATION, STATE_FINAL (terminal). Transition: INIT expects thought. After thought -> expect action. After action -> expect observation. After observation -> expect thought (new cycle) OR final. So final is only allowed when current state is after observation. We'll implement as a variable `next_allowed`.\n\nWe'll maintain a set of allowed types for current position.\n\nLet's do:\n\norder_map = {\n    0: {\"thought\"},  # start: only thought allowed\n    1: {\"thought\"},   # after observation? wait