at position after the observation, i.e., line index = len(lines) - 1? Actually if last line is observation, the expected next is thought or final, but no next line. The violation is that the required next is missing. The \"first violation\" would be at the end of the last line. In many parsers, you report the line where the error is detected, which could be the last line. I'll go with error_line = len(lines) (1-based). For example, if only one line \"Thought: hi\", then should be invalid because missing Action. The violation is at line 1? Actually line 1 is thought, which is valid alone, but the sequence requires Action after it. So the violation is that after line 1, Action is missing. So error_line = 1? But that line is valid. The description \"first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" They likely mean the line index where the violation occurred (i.e., the line that caused the violation). If the violation is missing a line, it's ambiguous. I'll check the typical ReAct parser task from other implementations. Possibly they expect that if the sequence is incomplete at the end, valid = false and error_line = len(lines) (the last line number). I'll adopt that.\n\nAlternatively, we can treat the absence of Final as a violation occurring at the end of the last line, so error_line = len(lines). That seems reasonable.\n\nNow, implement solve(case):\n\n- Define allowed prefixes: \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", \"Observation:\", \"Final:\"\n- Use a mapping from prefix_lower to type_lower: {\"thought\": \"thought\", \"action\": \"action\", \"observation\": \"observation\", \"final\": \"final\"}\n- But careful: prefix is case-sensitive? We'll match exactly the first word before colon. Use line.split(':', 1) and take the part before colon (strip whitespace) and check if it is exactly one of the allowed strings with correct case. The fixture has \"Thought:\" not \"thought:\". We'll enforce case-sensitive. If not one of the four, it's a violation.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case['lines']\n    trajectory = []\n    expected = ['thought']  # set as list for easier? Use set for allowed types\n    allowed = {'thought'}\n    error_line = None\n\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        # parse line\n        colon_index = line.find(':')\n        if colon_index == -1:\n            # no colon, invalid\n            error_line = i+1\n            break\n        prefix = line[:colon_index].strip()\n        content = line[colon_index+1:].strip()\n        # determine type from prefix\n        if prefix == 'Thought':\n            rec_type = 'thought'\n        elif prefix == 'Action':\n            rec_type = 'action'\n        elif prefix == 'Observation':\n            rec_type = 'observation'\n        elif prefix == 'Final':\n            rec_type = 'final'\n        else:\n            error_line = i+1\n            break\n\n        if rec_type not in allowed:\n            error_line = i+1\n            break\n        trajectory.append({'type': rec_type, 'content': content})\n        # update allowed next\n        if rec_type == 'thought':\n            allowed = {'action'}\n        elif rec_type == 'action':\n            allowed = {'observation'}\n        elif rec_type == 'observation':\n            allowed = {'thought', 'final'}\n        elif rec_type == 'final':\n            allowed = set()\n        # if rec_type final, no more allowed\n\n    if error_line is not None:\n        # we have violation, trajectory contains valid prefix (up to before violation)\n        return {'valid': False, 'error_line': error_line, 'trajectory': trajectory}\n    else:\n        # no violation during loop. Check if sequence ended properly.\n        # If allowed is empty, it ended with Final -> valid\n        # If allowed not empty, it's incomplete.\n        if not allowed:\n            return {'valid': True, 'error_line': None, 'trajectory': trajectory}\n        else:\n            # incomplete: missing required next type(s)\n            # Determine error_line: the last line number? We'll set to len(lines) (1-indexed last line)\n            error_line = len(lines)\n            # But the trajectory already contains all lines? The last line is valid, but the violation is after it.\n            # According to spec, trajectory should contain valid prefix. Since the last line is valid, we keep it.\n            # But the violation is after that line. So trajectory includes all lines.\n            return {'valid': False, 'error_line': error_line, 'trajectory': trajectory}\n\nBut we also need to consider case where there are no lines. In that case, loop doesn't run, error_line is None, allowed still {'thought'}. Then it's incomplete. We'll set error_line = 0? But line numbers start at 1. We could set error_line = 1 (the first missing line)? Or null? I'll set error_line = 1 (since the first required line is missing). But is there a line? No. The spec likely expects error_line to be line number of the first offending line, but if empty, there is no line. I'll set error_line = None? But that would make valid false and error_line null? The schema allows null. In that case, we could set error_line = null. But the spec says \"return its one-based line\". If no lines, it's unclear. I'll handle empty list as: valid false, error_line = None, trajectory = [].\n\nLet's modify: after loop, if len(lines)==0: return {'valid': False, 'error_line': None, 'trajectory': []}. Because no line to point to.\n\nAlso, if the first line is not thought, we break and trajectory is empty. That's fine.\n\nNow, test with fixture:\n\ncase1 lines: [\"Thought: inspect\",\"Action: lookup\",\"Observation: found\",\"Thought: answer\",\"Final: done\"]\nParsing:\ni0: thought -> allowed action\ni1: action -> allowed observation\ni2: observation -> allowed thought/final\ni3: thought -> allowed action\ni4: final -> allowed empty\nNo violation, allowed empty, valid true, error_line None, trajectory 5 items.\n\nSecond case same.\n\nThird